-- =============================================================================
-- V1 Baseline: shared database conventions.
--
-- Conventions used by every table in this schema:
--   * Primary keys are snowflake IDs generated by the application (assign_id),
--     never AUTO_INCREMENT, so IDs stay unique across future shards.
--   * Money is DECIMAL(19,4). NEVER use float/double for money.
--   * Quantities (tonnage, kg) are DECIMAL(18,3).
--   * Every business table carries created_at/updated_at/created_by/updated_by
--     plus a soft-delete flag `deleted` (0 = alive, 1 = deleted).
--   * updated_at is maintained by a trigger so the application can never forget
--     to bump it. Each migration writes its own, three lines per table:
--
--         CREATE TRIGGER trg_t_enterprise_updated_at
--             BEFORE UPDATE ON t_enterprise
--             FOR EACH ROW
--             SET NEW.updated_at = NOW(6);
--
--     No DELIMITER block is needed because the body is a single statement.
--
-- Ported from PostgreSQL. Three things changed shape, and knowing them makes
-- the rest of the schema readable:
--
--   1. TIMESTAMPTZ became DATETIME(6). MySQL has no timezone-aware type, so
--      the offset moved from the column into the connection — see the
--      connectionTimeZone parameter on the datasource URL. Every stored
--      instant is UTC by convention; what PG guaranteed per value, MySQL
--      guarantees per session.
--   2. jsonb became JSON. Same shape, fewer operators, and no untyped-parameter
--      trick — the driver binds a JSON string directly.
--   3. No index gets a `WHERE deleted = 0`. MySQL has no partial indexes, so
--      the soft-delete filter lives in the queries rather than in the index
--      definitions. The indexes that relied on it now lead with the columns
--      those queries filter on first.
-- =============================================================================

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- There was a helper procedure here, `attach_updated_at_trigger`, mirroring the
-- PostgreSQL trigger function that every table referenced. It is gone, and the
-- reason is worth keeping because the replacement is not obviously better:
-- writing three lines per table looks like duplication.
--
-- MySQL cannot execute `CALL` over the prepared-statement protocol Flyway uses,
-- so the procedure was unreachable from a migration. It could have been kept
-- for interactive use, but a helper that the migrations cannot call is a helper
-- that will drift from them — and this project has already been bitten twice by
-- a second copy of a rule.
--
-- So each migration declares its own triggers. Twelve short blocks that cannot
-- disagree with themselves beat one clever block that nothing calls.
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
